1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,219
Every evening, the till at the Corner Cafe
saves the day's sales as a file.

2
00:00:05,239 --> 00:00:08,079
On Friday, Asha adds four files up by
hand.

3
00:00:08,099 --> 00:00:09,210
It takes an hour.

4
00:00:09,233 --> 00:00:11,216
And the files are messy.

5
00:00:11,236 --> 00:00:12,885
One price is a word.

6
00:00:12,905 --> 00:00:15,222
One row has no price at all.

7
00:00:15,242 --> 00:00:16,724
One file is empty.

8
00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:20,650
Welcome to Chapter 25, the fifth boss
level!

9
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:26,493
Asha will build a report generator that
reads every file, survives the bad rows,

10
00:00:26,513 --> 00:00:28,310
and saves a clean report.

11
00:00:28,333 --> 00:00:32,706
An AI assistant will write part of it, and
make it look perfect.

12
00:00:32,726 --> 00:00:35,931
It will report nothing at all, and say
nothing.

13
00:00:35,951 --> 00:00:37,578
Her tests will catch it.

14
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:39,585
Here is the mission.

15
00:00:39,605 --> 00:00:42,130
Read every till file in one folder.

16
00:00:42,167 --> 00:00:46,417
Skip the bad rows, and count them, so Asha
knows how many.

17
00:00:46,467 --> 00:00:51,263
Keep the currency and the tax rate in a
settings file, so the owner can change

18
00:00:51,283 --> 00:00:53,177
them without touching the code.

19
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:56,365
And when something is wrong, say what is
wrong.

20
00:00:56,385 --> 00:00:57,178
Never crash.

21
00:00:57,787 --> 00:01:00,243
Every piece comes from Module 5.

22
00:01:00,263 --> 00:01:03,417
CSV files and text files from Chapter 21.

23
00:01:03,467 --> 00:01:08,727
Small try blocks, and raise value error
with a clear message, from Chapter 22.

24
00:01:08,767 --> 00:01:10,977
JSON from Chapter 23.

25
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:13,510
And from Chapter 24, a project folder.

26
00:01:13,530 --> 00:01:18,437
This project needs only the standard
library and pytest, so nothing new to

27
00:01:18,457 --> 00:01:18,970
install.

28
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:20,289
Plus one new tool.

29
00:01:20,309 --> 00:01:26,254
Glob finds every file in a folder whose
name matches a pattern, such as star dot c

30
00:01:26,274 --> 00:01:26,545
s v.

31
00:01:27,117 --> 00:01:29,097
Report tools holds the rules.

32
00:01:29,133 --> 00:01:33,233
Report dot p y runs the rules and saves
two reports.

33
00:01:33,267 --> 00:01:35,587
A test file checks the rules.

34
00:01:35,633 --> 00:01:40,443
The settings file holds the currency and
the tax rate: 8 percent.

35
00:01:40,467 --> 00:01:43,034
And the data folder holds the till files.

36
00:01:43,054 --> 00:01:48,021
Each name starts with a number, so the
days stay in order when they are sorted.

37
00:01:48,620 --> 00:01:50,703
Here is the finished report.

38
00:01:50,723 --> 00:01:51,980
One line per day.

39
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,940
The total, the tax, and the total with
tax.

40
00:01:54,967 --> 00:01:57,077
And how many rows were skipped.

41
00:01:57,100 --> 00:01:59,851
The program itself is about twenty lines.

42
00:01:59,871 --> 00:02:02,082
The rules live in the other file.

43
00:02:02,683 --> 00:02:04,843
Asha writes down what done means.

44
00:02:04,867 --> 00:02:05,195
One.

45
00:02:05,215 --> 00:02:09,457
Read every file, in day order, even an
empty one.

46
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:10,216
Two and three.

47
00:02:10,236 --> 00:02:14,380
Skip and count a row with a bad price or
no price, and refuse a negative price.

48
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:15,220
Four and five.

49
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:19,780
The settings come from a file, and a
problem with them gives one clear line.

50
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:20,850
Six and seven.

51
00:02:20,870 --> 00:02:24,750
The report, saved twice, and seven tests
that pass.

52
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:25,441
And eight.

53
00:02:25,461 --> 00:02:28,151
Nothing is rounded before it is added up.

54
00:02:28,753 --> 00:02:30,739
Quick check, from Chapter 22.

55
00:02:30,759 --> 00:02:32,744
The settings file is missing.

56
00:02:32,764 --> 00:02:34,472
What should a person see?

57
00:02:45,847 --> 00:02:45,967
D.

58
00:02:45,987 --> 00:02:48,696
One clear line that says what is wrong.

59
00:02:48,716 --> 00:02:52,966
A traceback is for developers, and silence
hides the problem.

60
00:02:53,587 --> 00:02:54,590
Take a breather.

61
00:02:54,610 --> 00:02:57,147
You know the files, and what done means.

62
00:02:57,167 --> 00:03:01,162
Next, Asha plans the functions with an AI
assistant.

63
00:03:05,883 --> 00:03:11,288
In this project, all six AI stages help
build one module: the report rules.

64
00:03:11,308 --> 00:03:12,301
Stage 1: plan.

65
00:03:12,321 --> 00:03:15,483
Asha asks for a plan, and says: no code
yet.

66
00:03:15,533 --> 00:03:21,483
The AI suggests one function that does
everything, inside one big try, with

67
00:03:21,503 --> 00:03:22,836
except Exception.

68
00:03:22,856 --> 00:03:25,223
Its promise: it never crashes.

69
00:03:25,267 --> 00:03:28,298
Asha checks it against Chapters 17 and 22.

70
00:03:28,318 --> 00:03:31,421
One big function cannot be tested in
parts.

71
00:03:31,441 --> 00:03:37,087
And except Exception hides the real
problem: the program never crashes, but it

72
00:03:37,107 --> 00:03:38,177
never says why.

73
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:43,702
So she asks for small functions with one
job each, and a small try around the one

74
00:03:43,722 --> 00:03:44,997
line that can fail.

75
00:03:45,583 --> 00:03:46,581
Time to build.

76
00:03:46,601 --> 00:03:50,435
Step 1: before any rule, Asha looks at the
real data.

77
00:03:50,455 --> 00:03:53,343
She prints two of the files, row by row.

78
00:03:53,367 --> 00:03:57,611
Every till file starts with the same line:
item comma amount.

79
00:03:57,631 --> 00:04:00,757
Those are the column names, in small
letters.

80
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:06,414
Line 6 joins the folder and the file name
with a slash, as in Chapter 21.

81
00:04:06,434 --> 00:04:11,740
Line 8 reads each row as a dictionary,
with the column names as keys.

82
00:04:11,767 --> 00:04:13,297
One rule to know first.

83
00:04:13,317 --> 00:04:18,961
When a row is shorter than the first line,
Dict reader fills each missing value with

84
00:04:18,981 --> 00:04:19,298
None.

85
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:33,570
3 wednesday dot c s v, cake, None.

86
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:36,740
And on Tuesday, the amount is the word
four.

87
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:37,961
Two damaged rows.

88
00:04:37,981 --> 00:04:40,259
Every good amount is still text.

89
00:04:40,850 --> 00:04:42,505
Step 2: the settings.

90
00:04:42,525 --> 00:04:45,456
Load config has two small try blocks.

91
00:04:45,476 --> 00:04:47,450
The first reads the file.

92
00:04:47,470 --> 00:04:50,880
A missing file raises file not found
error.

93
00:04:50,900 --> 00:04:56,764
Line 12 turns it into a value error with a
message a person can act on: missing

94
00:04:56,784 --> 00:04:58,850
settings file, and its name.

95
00:04:58,900 --> 00:05:01,371
The second try reads the JSON.

96
00:05:01,391 --> 00:05:04,775
A broken file raises a JSON decode error.

97
00:05:04,795 --> 00:05:08,760
Line 16 turns it into one clear value
error too.

98
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:12,839
Error dot msg is the short reason from the
JSON module.

99
00:05:12,859 --> 00:05:17,119
So the main program needs to catch only
one kind of error.

100
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:23,280
When the file is fine, the settings come
back as a dictionary.

101
00:05:23,300 --> 00:05:28,150
The currency is U S D, and the tax rate is
0.08, which is 8 percent.

102
00:05:28,750 --> 00:05:31,005
Step 3: one amount at a time.

103
00:05:31,025 --> 00:05:33,593
Asha writes parse amount herself.

104
00:05:33,613 --> 00:05:37,044
Line 21: float turns the text into a
number.

105
00:05:37,064 --> 00:05:40,260
A word already raises a value error here.

106
00:05:40,300 --> 00:05:46,734
Lines 22 and 23: a price below zero is
refused, with its own value error.

107
00:05:47,353 --> 00:05:52,353
She tries three amounts: 3.50, the word
four, and minus 2.

108
00:05:52,373 --> 00:05:57,718
Line 7 catches the value error, and line 8
prints its message.

109
00:06:09,080 --> 00:06:11,929
Minus 2 refused: negative amount.

110
00:06:11,949 --> 00:06:15,320
The message from line 23 of the module.

111
00:06:15,367 --> 00:06:19,775
And the word four is refused by float,
with Python's own message.

112
00:06:20,387 --> 00:06:21,467
Stage 2: write.

113
00:06:21,487 --> 00:06:23,595
Asha wrote her version first.

114
00:06:23,615 --> 00:06:27,557
Now she asks the AI for its own, without
showing hers.

115
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:31,616
The AI's version is one line, with strip
for spaces.

116
00:06:31,636 --> 00:06:35,963
Asha renames it ai version, so both can
sit in one file.

117
00:06:36,553 --> 00:06:41,053
She runs both on the same three amounts.

118
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:44,190
3.50 and 2.75 with a space: both agree.

119
00:06:44,233 --> 00:06:45,349
But minus 2.

120
00:06:45,369 --> 00:06:48,663
The AI's version returns minus 2.0.

121
00:06:48,683 --> 00:06:50,273
A negative price.

122
00:06:50,293 --> 00:06:51,787
Hers refuses it.

123
00:06:51,807 --> 00:06:53,586
Asha keeps her own.

124
00:06:54,187 --> 00:06:54,757
Breather.

125
00:06:54,777 --> 00:06:56,134
Relax your shoulders.

126
00:06:56,154 --> 00:06:58,627
The settings and the amounts are safe.

127
00:06:58,667 --> 00:07:03,531
Next, Asha writes her tests, and lets the
AI write the reading code.

128
00:07:08,283 --> 00:07:08,774
Step 4.

129
00:07:08,794 --> 00:07:12,127
As in Chapter 20, Asha writes the tests
first.

130
00:07:12,147 --> 00:07:15,334
Four check the amounts and the money
format.

131
00:07:15,354 --> 00:07:17,593
These two check the whole week.

132
00:07:17,633 --> 00:07:23,603
The week total must be 37.25, and exactly
two rows must be skipped.

133
00:07:23,633 --> 00:07:27,230
And Tuesday must be 11.25, not rounded.

134
00:07:27,853 --> 00:07:29,186
Stage 3: generate.

135
00:07:29,206 --> 00:07:33,095
Asha pastes her tests and asks for the
reading code.

136
00:07:33,115 --> 00:07:34,823
Do not change my tests.

137
00:07:34,867 --> 00:07:40,690
The AI writes read sales, and says: it
skips only the two damaged rows, so your

138
00:07:40,710 --> 00:07:41,503
tests pass.

139
00:07:41,523 --> 00:07:42,613
It sounds sure.

140
00:07:43,220 --> 00:07:44,483
Look at line 33.

141
00:07:44,503 --> 00:07:51,138
Then remember the first line of every till
file, from step 1: item comma amount, in

142
00:07:51,158 --> 00:07:52,260
small letters.

143
00:07:52,300 --> 00:07:55,551
And line 35 catches every kind of
exception.

144
00:08:06,913 --> 00:08:10,141
Line 33 asks for Amount, with a capital A.

145
00:08:10,161 --> 00:08:14,317
There is no such key, so every row raises
a key error.

146
00:08:14,337 --> 00:08:16,637
And except Exception hides it.

147
00:08:17,253 --> 00:08:18,652
Asha does not argue.

148
00:08:18,672 --> 00:08:21,913
She counts the good and bad rows in each
file.

149
00:08:21,933 --> 00:08:22,619
Zero good.

150
00:08:22,639 --> 00:08:23,820
Every row is bad.

151
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:27,985
The program ran with no error, and it
would report nothing.

152
00:08:28,587 --> 00:08:30,034
Her six tests agree.

153
00:08:30,054 --> 00:08:33,410
The first four pass, and both week tests
fail.

154
00:08:34,020 --> 00:08:39,721
Asha fixes it with the rule from Chapter
22: catch only the exceptions you expect.

155
00:08:39,741 --> 00:08:43,140
Line 33: the right key, and her own parse
amount.

156
00:08:43,167 --> 00:08:46,643
Line 34 catches only the two exceptions
she expects.

157
00:08:46,663 --> 00:08:48,659
Value error for the word four.

158
00:08:48,679 --> 00:08:54,038
And type error for the missing price,
because float of None raises a type error,

159
00:08:54,058 --> 00:08:56,727
the same as the short row in Chapter 22.

160
00:08:56,767 --> 00:09:01,291
Line 37 adds the sale only when nothing
went wrong, in else.

161
00:09:01,887 --> 00:09:03,995
Now Monday has four good rows.

162
00:09:04,015 --> 00:09:07,117
Tuesday and Wednesday each have one bad
row.

163
00:09:07,137 --> 00:09:08,394
Thursday is empty.

164
00:09:08,987 --> 00:09:11,351
And she runs all six tests again.

165
00:09:11,371 --> 00:09:12,146
Six passed.

166
00:09:12,753 --> 00:09:13,593
Quick check.

167
00:09:13,613 --> 00:09:16,601
A row has no price, so its amount is None.

168
00:09:16,621 --> 00:09:19,538
Which exception does float of None raise?

169
00:09:30,913 --> 00:09:31,038
C.

170
00:09:31,058 --> 00:09:31,836
Type error.

171
00:09:31,856 --> 00:09:34,156
None is the wrong type of value.

172
00:09:34,176 --> 00:09:39,013
A word such as four is a string, so it
gives a value error instead.

173
00:09:39,583 --> 00:09:40,130
Step 5.

174
00:09:40,150 --> 00:09:42,722
Build week puts it all together.

175
00:09:42,742 --> 00:09:46,853
Line 44: glob finds every file ending in
dot c s v.

176
00:09:46,873 --> 00:09:51,793
Glob gives no fixed order, so sorted puts
them in name order.

177
00:09:51,833 --> 00:09:54,015
Line 45 reads each file.

178
00:09:54,035 --> 00:09:57,593
Line 46 adds its bad rows to the count.

179
00:09:57,633 --> 00:10:04,598
Lines 48 to 51 add each amount to its item
and to the day, with get, as in Chapter

180
00:10:04,618 --> 00:10:04,853
12.

181
00:10:04,900 --> 00:10:08,412
Line 52 makes the day name from the file
name.

182
00:10:08,432 --> 00:10:14,862
Stem drops dot c s v, split takes the part
after the dash, and title gives a capital

183
00:10:14,882 --> 00:10:15,400
letter.

184
00:10:16,020 --> 00:10:16,764
Quick check.

185
00:10:16,784 --> 00:10:19,627
Why does build week sort the paths from
glob?

186
00:10:30,980 --> 00:10:31,103
A.

187
00:10:31,123 --> 00:10:32,962
Glob gives no fixed order.

188
00:10:32,982 --> 00:10:38,467
Sorted puts the names in order, and the
number at the start keeps the days in

189
00:10:38,487 --> 00:10:38,896
order.

190
00:10:39,487 --> 00:10:45,347
Asha prints the days as JSON, with an
indent of two, as in Chapter 23.

191
00:10:45,367 --> 00:10:49,686
Thursday's file is empty, so build week
adds nothing for it.

192
00:11:01,047 --> 00:11:01,685
37.25.

193
00:11:01,705 --> 00:11:06,627
14, plus 11.25, plus 12, plus 0 for
Thursday.

194
00:11:06,667 --> 00:11:09,961
And two rows skipped.

195
00:11:10,553 --> 00:11:12,228
The main program is short.

196
00:11:12,248 --> 00:11:17,182
The try block holds two lines, and both
raise only value error, with a clear

197
00:11:17,202 --> 00:11:17,703
message.

198
00:11:17,733 --> 00:11:23,443
Lines 10 and 11 catch it once, and print
Report stopped, and the message.

199
00:11:23,467 --> 00:11:26,771
The else block runs only when both worked.

200
00:11:26,791 --> 00:11:30,887
Lines 13 to 15 write the report, one line
at a time.

201
00:11:30,933 --> 00:11:33,358
Lines 16 and 17 save the week as JSON.

202
00:11:33,378 --> 00:11:38,698
And line 18 reads the text report back, so
the screen shows exactly what was saved.

203
00:11:39,320 --> 00:11:39,920
Breather.

204
00:11:39,940 --> 00:11:42,260
The report works on the real week.

205
00:11:42,300 --> 00:11:45,699
Next, Asha tries to break it with new test
data.

206
00:11:50,450 --> 00:11:51,622
Stage 4: test.

207
00:11:51,642 --> 00:11:57,840
Asha asks the AI for realistic test data,
in the same format as her till.

208
00:11:57,867 --> 00:12:02,576
The AI gives her a small file, and says it
is in her till's format.

209
00:12:03,187 --> 00:12:07,977
Asha compares the first line with a real
till file, letter by letter.

210
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:12,215
The real file starts item comma amount, in
small letters.

211
00:12:12,235 --> 00:12:14,593
Now look at the AI's first line.

212
00:12:25,947 --> 00:12:27,880
It crashes with a key error.

213
00:12:27,900 --> 00:12:32,834
The AI's columns start with capital
letters, so there is no key amount.

214
00:12:32,854 --> 00:12:37,718
And her except catches only value error
and type error, not key error.

215
00:12:37,738 --> 00:12:42,114
Asha saves the file as a fifth day, and
runs the report to see.

216
00:12:42,720 --> 00:12:43,508
It crashes.

217
00:12:43,528 --> 00:12:44,390
A key error.

218
00:12:44,410 --> 00:12:48,210
Read the last line first: the key amount
is missing.

219
00:12:48,233 --> 00:12:53,684
Then the last file line shows where: line
33 of report tools, inside read sales.

220
00:12:53,704 --> 00:12:57,923
On your computer, these lines show the
full path of each file.

221
00:12:57,967 --> 00:13:01,986
Her except catches value error and type
error, not key error.

222
00:13:02,006 --> 00:13:06,556
That is right: a whole file in the wrong
format is not a damaged row.

223
00:13:07,153 --> 00:13:08,225
Stage 5: debug.

224
00:13:08,245 --> 00:13:13,683
Asha pastes the error, and asks for the
cause first, then the smallest fix.

225
00:13:13,733 --> 00:13:18,928
The AI explains: Dict reader uses the
first line as the keys, exactly as

226
00:13:18,948 --> 00:13:19,507
written.

227
00:13:19,527 --> 00:13:22,983
Its fix checks the column names before the
loop.

228
00:13:23,033 --> 00:13:26,340
Field names is the list of names from the
first line.

229
00:13:26,360 --> 00:13:30,105
An empty file has none, so if columns lets
Thursday through.

230
00:13:30,720 --> 00:13:33,501
She runs the report on the same file.

231
00:13:33,521 --> 00:13:37,059
One clear line: the file needs item and
amount.

232
00:13:37,079 --> 00:13:37,740
No crash.

233
00:13:38,353 --> 00:13:42,928
Asha keeps the AI's file as test data, and
adds a seventh test.

234
00:13:42,948 --> 00:13:46,063
Read sales must raise a value error for
it.

235
00:13:46,100 --> 00:13:48,602
Then she runs every test again.

236
00:13:48,622 --> 00:13:49,659
Seven passed.

237
00:13:50,253 --> 00:13:54,089
Last, Asha runs the whole report on three
more folders.

238
00:13:54,109 --> 00:13:56,683
She writes the expected result first.

239
00:13:56,733 --> 00:13:58,883
No settings file at all.

240
00:13:58,933 --> 00:14:01,803
A settings file that is missing a comma.

241
00:14:01,833 --> 00:14:06,641
And a week where the cafe was closed every
day but Thursday's empty file.

242
00:14:07,253 --> 00:14:08,708
No settings file.

243
00:14:08,728 --> 00:14:10,009
One clear line.

244
00:14:10,029 --> 00:14:10,442
Pass.

245
00:14:11,053 --> 00:14:12,346
Broken settings.

246
00:14:12,366 --> 00:14:15,956
The JSON module's reason: expecting a
comma.

247
00:14:15,976 --> 00:14:16,366
Pass.

248
00:14:16,987 --> 00:14:18,873
And the closed week.

249
00:14:18,893 --> 00:14:22,684
Only Thursday, and every amount is 0.00.

250
00:14:22,704 --> 00:14:23,161
Pass.

251
00:14:23,181 --> 00:14:24,686
All three match.

252
00:14:25,283 --> 00:14:26,536
Stage 6: improve.

253
00:14:26,556 --> 00:14:28,108
All seven tests pass.

254
00:14:28,128 --> 00:14:33,723
Asha asks for one improvement, and says:
do not change what the totals are.

255
00:14:33,767 --> 00:14:39,162
The AI says: round each day's total to a
whole number before you store it.

256
00:14:39,182 --> 00:14:40,114
Tidy amounts.

257
00:14:40,720 --> 00:14:43,937
Here is the AI's change, on line 56.

258
00:14:43,957 --> 00:14:46,634
Tuesday's real total is 11.25.

259
00:14:46,654 --> 00:14:53,468
Round with no second value gives the
nearest whole number, as in Chapter 18.

260
00:15:04,847 --> 00:15:05,136
11.

261
00:15:05,156 --> 00:15:09,357
And the week becomes 37 instead of 37.25.

262
00:15:09,377 --> 00:15:12,550
Asha runs her tests to be sure.

263
00:15:13,153 --> 00:15:17,713
Two tests fail: the week total, and the
day that must not be rounded.

264
00:15:17,733 --> 00:15:21,428
The suggestion broke her rule: it changed
the totals.

265
00:15:21,448 --> 00:15:26,754
Rounding belongs in the display, and money
already shows two decimal places.

266
00:15:26,774 --> 00:15:28,365
Asha keeps her version.

267
00:15:28,987 --> 00:15:32,247
Here are all six stages, on one module of
rules.

268
00:15:32,267 --> 00:15:36,347
The AI helped at every stage, from the
plan to the improvement.

269
00:15:36,367 --> 00:15:42,195
And it was confidently wrong five times:
the plan, a negative price, a hidden key

270
00:15:42,215 --> 00:15:45,877
error, test data in the wrong format, and
rounding.

271
00:15:45,900 --> 00:15:50,365
Every time, something Asha did first
caught it: looking at real files,

272
00:15:50,385 --> 00:15:51,967
comparing, and her tests.

273
00:15:52,587 --> 00:15:53,550
Last breather.

274
00:15:53,570 --> 00:15:58,817
Before she pasted any code into the chat,
Asha checked it for private data.

275
00:15:58,837 --> 00:16:01,837
The till files hold only items and
amounts.

276
00:16:01,867 --> 00:16:02,881
Let's recap.

277
00:16:07,617 --> 00:16:09,877
Here is what you used in this boss level.

278
00:16:09,900 --> 00:16:11,899
Look at the real data first.

279
00:16:11,919 --> 00:16:13,630
Damaged rows are normal.

280
00:16:13,667 --> 00:16:17,827
Small functions, and a small try around
the one line that can fail.

281
00:16:17,867 --> 00:16:20,022
Catch the exceptions you expect.

282
00:16:20,042 --> 00:16:22,197
Except Exception can hide a bug.

283
00:16:22,233 --> 00:16:26,843
Turn file and JSON errors into one clear
message, and catch it once.

284
00:16:26,867 --> 00:16:31,617
Check AI test data against a real file,
and keep it as a test.

285
00:16:31,667 --> 00:16:34,226
And round only when you display a number.

286
00:16:34,820 --> 00:16:36,149
One last check.

287
00:16:36,169 --> 00:16:39,118
Monday is 2.4 and Tuesday is 2.4.

288
00:16:39,138 --> 00:16:42,267
Which line shows the correct total?

289
00:16:53,647 --> 00:16:53,798
B.

290
00:16:53,818 --> 00:16:56,617
Add the exact amounts first: 4.8.

291
00:16:56,637 --> 00:16:59,779
Then show it with two decimal places.

292
00:17:00,387 --> 00:17:01,983
Boss level complete!

293
00:17:02,003 --> 00:17:05,699
You earned 300 XP and the Report Master
badge.

294
00:17:05,719 --> 00:17:07,396
And Module 5 is done.

295
00:17:09,487 --> 00:17:13,967
The practice file has eight more tasks,
worth 170 XP.

296
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:18,410
Three warm-ups, worth 10 XP each, such as
reading one till file.

297
00:17:18,433 --> 00:17:22,869
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each,
including a clear message for missing

298
00:17:22,889 --> 00:17:23,433
settings.

299
00:17:23,467 --> 00:17:26,001
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

300
00:17:26,021 --> 00:17:30,627
In the last one, you fix an AI's report
that rounds before it adds.

301
00:17:30,667 --> 00:17:33,555
Look at your data first, then write the
rule.

302
00:17:34,150 --> 00:17:39,411
Asha's report keeps each sale as a pair of
an item and an amount, and each day as a

303
00:17:39,431 --> 00:17:40,111
dictionary.

304
00:17:40,131 --> 00:17:42,020
The rules live somewhere else.

305
00:17:42,067 --> 00:17:47,577
In Chapter 26, you will build your own
types with classes, so a sale or a day

306
00:17:47,597 --> 00:17:50,737
carries its data and its own rules
together.

307
00:17:50,767 --> 00:17:52,459
Do the warm-up tasks now.

308
00:17:52,479 --> 00:17:54,377
They take about ten minutes.

309
00:17:54,400 --> 00:17:56,485
See you in Chapter 26.
